Patch summary:
A task's surface stays shown while a child activity is requested visible but
not yet committed so. When an app's first activity finishes as its next one
starts (3DMark's splash hands over to its main activity while the task's open
animation still plays), the finishing activity is committed invisible as the
transitions that animate the task finish, but the starting one is committed
visible only once its own, later transition is ready. The frame between the
two found no visible child in the task and hid the task surface, and the later
transition shows only the activity, never the task again: the window stayed
black and took no input although WM reported it drawn and visible. The task's
surface visibility now also follows the requested visibility of its children.

diff --git a/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/Task.java b/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/Task.java
--- a/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/Task.java
+++ b/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/Task.java
@@ -3385,7 +3385,13 @@ class Task extends TaskFragment {
 
     @Override
     void updateSurfaceVisibility(SurfaceControl.Transaction t) {
-        t.setVisibility(mSurfaceControl, isVisible());
+        // Ika: a task stays shown while a child is requested visible but not yet committed so.
+        // When an activity finishes as the next one of the task starts, the finishing one is
+        // committed invisible as the transitions that still animate the task finish, while the
+        // starting one is committed visible only once its own transition is ready; the frame in
+        // between found no visible child, hid the task surface, and the later transition shows
+        // only the activity, so the window stayed black (3DMark's splash on an app-window display).
+        t.setVisibility(mSurfaceControl, isVisible() || isVisibleRequested());
     }
 
     @Override
